Postmortem 
  Our original idea was a horror/anomaly genre, which we discussed during our first meeting. Unfortunately, we received the verb list after that session, so we had to brainstorm some more ideas to fit the verbs we rolled. We then landed on “sing” and “watch,” and eventually came up with a rhythm game idea (a cross between Smule and Piano Tiles). But as we started the process of actually making it in Godot, we realized the concept wasn't so beginner-friendly, as mapping (and importing) the beats/music to user input was complicated. The issue of the music not being open source deterred us as well. So, we decided to shift gears and roll again, landing on “bounce” and “skip.” This time, we decided to move forward and create a platform game. 

  After a YouTube video or two, the sprites and collision physics went pretty well, as they weren't very hard to understand. Then we figured out how to make tiles from assets; the platforms were easy to make, and the repetition made us more and more familiar with Godot. The character design also went well; we found an open-source tool to create pixel art sprites, and the animation and movement for it (again after some tutorials) weren’t difficult at all. After spending time with Godot, we agreed that the hardest part about it wasn’t learning new things (or even the code), but troubleshooting it and managing the small configurations you have to set for every single thing you do. Since we started production late, we ran out of time to implement features we wanted, like an enemy, a points system, or more levels.
Playtesting gave us a lot of valuable feedback that we used to improve our game. A lot of playtesters said that our theme was not cohesive/didn’t tell a story, and we received feedback on our player physics. Before playtesting, we were very focused on the collision physics/script, as the many idea changes had taken a lot of time, so we just wanted to have a playable version of the game. After playtesting, we realized how important the story and visual part of the game were, so we took the time to find assets that were more cohesive and implemented them (in the GitHub, we actually still have the old tileset that we used for playtesting as a hidden layer). We also took the time to change the character physics/gravity, making the UX better. 

  Another problem we had was organizing a time for everyone to meet since we ended up having conflicting and/or heavy schedules. The one thing we all agreed made this project the most difficult was figuring out a way all three of us could use Godot. We are all mostly unfamiliar with GitHub, so initially we stayed away from it and used a shared Google Drive to store the assets. As a result, we could only make meaningful progress when we met in person and used the laptop of the person who had the project. Towards the end, we decided to try to figure out GitHub, and with some research, surprisingly, it was easy to set up; we regretted not doing it earlier. From making this game, we learned how to use Godot at a decent level, again through YouTube tutorials. We also learned a lot about cooperation and coordination when working with other people on semi-complex software. For some of us, it was our first college group project, and we all enjoyed creating this game together. Moreover, making this game created a newfound respect for game designers; even just making one level for a game can be incredibly complex and difficult. However, the feeling when we sat down and played our completed game made it worth it. 

  After having made a game, there are some things we would do differently. We spent a lot of time thinking of what we wanted to do, trying to find the perfect idea, but looking back, we realized that it’s better to commit to an unfinished idea early and refine as you go, so you don’t rush production. 
